      "name": "ConvertBytePairsToArgbPixels",
      "signature": "void ConvertBytePairsToArgbPixels(int nPixelPairCount)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Convert byte pairs to ARGB pixel format with alpha blending constant\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize loop counter from pixel pair count parameter (param_1 << 1)\n2. Load current source and destination buffer pointers from globals\n3. First loop - Process from current pointers forward:\n   a. Read two bytes from destination buffer at current position\n   b. Advance source buffer pointer by 8 bytes (2 DWORDs)\n   c. Advance destination buffer pointer by 2 bytes\n   d. Convert each byte to ARGB format: (byte << 0x18) | 0x800080 | (byte << 0x8)\n   e. Store two ARGB DWORDs to source buffer\n   f. Update global current pointers\n   g. Decrement counter and repeat if non-zero\n4. Reset loop counter from pixel pair count parameter (param_1 << 1)\n5. Second loop - Process from end pointers backward:\n   a. Read two bytes from destination buffer at end position\n   b. Advance source buffer end pointer by 8 bytes (2 DWORDs)\n   c. Advance destination buffer end pointer by 2 bytes\n   d. Convert each byte to ARGB format: (byte << 0x18) | 0x800080 | (byte << 0x8)\n   e. Store two ARGB DWORDs to source buffer at end position\n   f. Update global end pointers\n   g. Decrement counter and repeat if non-zero\n\nParameters:\nnPixelPairCount (int): Number of pixel pairs to process in each direction\n\nReturns:\nvoid: No return value\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x800080: ARGB alpha blending constant (0x80 alpha, 0x00 red, 0x80 blue)\n0x18: Left shift count for alpha channel positioning (bits 31-24)\n0x8: Left shift count for green channel positioning (bits 15-8)\n\nSpecial Cases:\nFunction processes data bidirectionally from both current and end buffer pointers\nEach byte pair generates two ARGB DWORDs with identical color values\nSource buffer receives converted ARGB pixels, destination buffer provides raw bytes\n\nNote: Function uses 1 stack-allocated temporary variable optimized away by decompiler",
