      "name": "ProcessSignalArrays",
      "signature": "void ProcessSignalArrays(int nLength, int nMode, float * pfInputBuffer, int * pnControlArray, float * pfOutputBuffer)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "ProcessSignalArrays - Process signal arrays with conditional mode-based operations\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate buffer capacity by comparing control array size with input length\n2. If insufficient capacity (*pnControlArray * 4 < nLength), expand using FUN_10017e40\n3. For arrays larger than 4 elements, choose processing mode based on nMode parameter\n4. If nMode >= 0 (positive mode): Call FUN_10017f20 then FUN_10018300 for forward processing\n5. If nMode < 0 (negative mode): Call FUN_100180e0 then FUN_10018480 for reverse processing\n6. For exactly 4 elements, perform direct processing with FUN_10018300\n7. Return without processing for arrays smaller than 4 elements\n\nParameters:\n- nLength (int): Length of data to process (must be > 0)\n- nMode (int): Processing mode flag (-1 for reverse, >= 0 for forward)\n- pfInputBuffer (float*): Input array buffer containing signal data\n- pnControlArray (int*): Control array with capacity info in first element\n- pfOutputBuffer (float*): Output array buffer for processed results\n\nReturns:\n- void: Function performs in-place operations on provided buffers\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Arrays with length <= 4 use optimized direct processing path\n- Buffer capacity validation prevents overflow conditions\n- Mode selection affects algorithm choice for signal processing direction\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n- 0x4 (4): Minimum array size threshold for complex processing\n- 0x2 (2): Right shift amount for capacity calculation (divide by 4)\n- -1: Mode flag indicating reverse/negative processing direction",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.07",
      "method": "CAL",
      "index": "CAL:61f46ce206bebc08275ecff715918237",
