      "name": "GenerateBitReversedIndices",
      "signature": "void GenerateBitReversedIndices(int nArrayLength, int * pnIndexArray, float * pfDataBuffer)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Generate bit-reversed indices for FFT preprocessing and perform data swapping\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize index array with zero at first element and set step size to 1\n2. Generate bit-reversed index pattern by iterating through power-of-2 step sizes\n3. For each step size, fill upper half of index array with lower half values plus current step\n4. Double the step size and continue until all indices are generated\n5. Determine swapping mode based on array size and step alignment\n6. If step size * 4 equals or exceeds array length, use dual-pointer swapping mode\n7. If step size * 4 is less than array length, use single-pointer swapping mode\n8. For dual-pointer mode, swap float pairs at computed indices with extended stride\n9. For single-pointer mode, swap float pairs at computed indices with basic stride\n10. Continue swapping iterations until all bit-reversed pairs are exchanged\n\nParameters:\n- nArrayLength (int): Length of the signal array to process (must be power of 2 and > 4)\n- pnIndexArray (int*): Pre-allocated index array for storing bit-reversed indices\n- pfDataBuffer (float*): Signal data buffer containing complex values as float pairs\n\nReturns:\n- void: Function performs in-place bit-reversal on pfDataBuffer using generated indices\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Arrays with length <= 4 are handled by calling function before this routine\n- Index generation phase creates bit-reversal pattern for efficient FFT processing\n- Swapping mode selection optimizes memory access patterns based on array size\n- Float pairs represent complex numbers (real, imaginary) processed as consecutive values\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n- 0x4 (4): Minimum array size threshold for index generation\n- 0x1 (1): Initial step size for bit-reversal pattern generation\n- 0x2 (2): Step increment for offset progression and stride calculations\n- 0x8 (8): Bit shift threshold for determining step size termination (step_size * 8)\n\nError Handling:\n- Function assumes valid power-of-2 array length provided by caller\n- No boundary validation performed - relies on ProcessSignalArrays for safety checks\n- Index array must be pre-allocated with sufficient capacity by calling function\n\nState Machine:\n- State 1: Index Generation - Build bit-reversal pattern in index array\n- State 2: Mode Selection - Choose swapping algorithm based on size alignment\n- State 3A: Dual-Pointer Swapping - Exchange float pairs using two pointer arrays\n- State 3B: Single-Pointer Swapping - Exchange float pairs using one pointer array\n- State 4: Completion - All bit-reversed pairs swapped, ready for FFT processing",
